scription_full":"Viking Therapeutics, Inc., a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company, focuses on the development of novel therapies for metabolic and endocrine disorders. Its lead drug candidate is VK2809, an orally available tissue and receptor-subtype selective agonist of the thyroid hormone receptor beta (TR\u00df), which is in Phase IIb clinical trials to treat patients with biopsy-confirmed non-alcoholic steatohepatitis, as well as NAFLD. The company develops VK5211, an orally available non-steroidal selective androgen receptor modulator that is in Phase II clinical trials for the treatment of patients recovering from non-elective hip fracture surgery; VK0612, which is in Phase II clinical trials for metabolic disorders and anemia; VK2735, a novel dual agonist of the glucagon-like peptide 1, which is in Phase 1 SAD/MAD clinical trial, and VK0214, an orally available tissue and receptor-subtype selective agonist of the TR\u00df for X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy. The company was incorporated in 2012 and is headquartered in San Diego, California.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Brian  Lian Ph.D. | President, CEO & Director | Age 59 | Compensation: $1.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 74 people. Stock-based compensation of $552K per employee reflects an ownership culture. Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Viking Therapeutics, Inc. is a mid-cap healthcare business with a $3.4B market cap on $0 of annual revenue.","Returns on capital run at -287% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","Accruals are elevated at -11.3% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.","On the balance sheet, interest coverage of 40\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 2.8% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotr